Short Summary of Chapter 3: Ethereum Nodes
An Ethereum node is software that follows Ethereum's rules and communicates with other nodes on the network.
After The Merge (2022), a node requires two clients:
Execution Client: Executes transactions and smart contracts in the EVM.
Consensus Client: Maintains consensus using Proof of Stake (PoS).
Popular execution clients include Geth, Nethermind, Besu, Erigon, and Reth. Popular consensus clients include Prysm, Lighthouse, Teku, Nimbus, and Lodestar.
Full nodes download and validate blockchain data independently, improving Ethereum's security and censorship resistance.
Archive nodes store all historical blockchain data, while full nodes prune older state data to save space.
Running a full node has benefits (privacy, independence, direct blockchain access) but requires significant storage, bandwidth, and maintenance.
Alternatives for development include:
Public testnets (e.g., Sepolia)
Local blockchain simulations (e.g., Anvil)
Hosted node providers (e.g., Infura, Alchemy).
To run a mainnet full node, recommended hardware includes:
4+ core CPU
16+ GB RAM
2+ TB NVMe SSD
Fast internet connection.
The chapter demonstrates setting up Ethereum nodes using:
Geth + Prysm
Reth + Lighthouse.
Ethereum clients expose a JSON-RPC API, allowing applications to interact with the blockchain programmatically. Common methods include retrieving client versions and gas prices.
Remote clients (wallets) like MetaMask, Rabby, Phantom, and Coinbase Wallet rely on full nodes instead of storing the blockchain themselves.
Future Ethereum improvements include history pruning, Verkle trees, statelessness, and zk-EVM technologie
